Dataset cf14071_repro {
  label: 'CF-14071 repro (customer diagram)'
  description: '''
    The customer's diagram, box for box and line for line. 8 models, 8 relationships, nothing else.

      Dim Store            -> Fact Sales, Fact SOH
      Dim Region           -> Fact Sales, Fact SOH
      Dim Date             -> Fact Sales, Fact Sales (Benchmark)
      Dim Region (Benchmark) -> Fact Sales (Benchmark), Fact SOH (Benchmark)

    The RLS rule is on Dim Store. There is deliberately NO relationship from Dim Store to either benchmark
    fact - the diagram's "No Path" arrow.

    EVERY RELATIONSHIP IS 'one_way' on filter_direction, with rlp_propagation left UNSET. This is the
    standard galaxy-schema setting: users filter/group facts by dimensions (dim -> fact) but cannot combine
    benchmark metrics with core store dimensions.

    THIS DATASET DOES NOT RAISE THE CUSTOMER'S ERROR, and that turns out to be correct behaviour.

    Per Khai (2026-09-04), the docs are wrong and he is updating them: when rlp_propagation is OMITTED it is
    'two_way' by DEFAULT - it does NOT inherit filter_direction. The reachability error is only raised when
    rlp_propagation is explicitly forced to 'one_way'. See cf14071_repro_rlp_oneway for that variant.

    So here RLP propagation is two_way, every model is reachable from the rule, and there is nothing to
    complain about. Do not read the absence of an error as a bug.

    OBSERVED, in production as an Explorer with vendor_id = 1:
      dim_store lookup           -> only store 1, so the rule binds and RLP is enforced
      Benchmark Sales $          -> 20500, the FULL cohort, unfiltered, no error
      Sales $ + Benchmark Sales $ together -> no error

    That is exactly the behaviour the customer asked for: core restricted, benchmark whole, both in one
    report. No synthetic -1 member and no out-of-scope marker required.

    ONE LOOSE END worth raising internally. If RLP propagation really is two_way by default, the store
    filter should have travelled Dim Store -> Fact Sales -> UP into Dim Date -> DOWN into Fact Sales
    (Benchmark), narrowing the benchmark to store 1's only date (20260102) and giving 13000 (7000 + 6000).
    It returned 20500 instead. So either RLP does not traverse through an intermediate FACT table, or
    two_way propagation governs reachability without applying the filter along that path.

    Fixtures. Stores 1-5 (Store A-E); Fact SOH only carries stores 1-3.
    With vendor_id = 1 (Store A): Sales $ = 300, SOH Qty = 10, Benchmark Sales $ = 20500.

    RLP as-code is production-only - not enforced in the dev workspace at all - and Admins plus Analysts
    with data-source access bypass it. Publish and test as an Explorer or Viewer.
  '''
  data_source_name: 'demodb'

  models: [
    dim_store,
    dim_region,
    dim_date,
    dim_region_benchmark,
    fact_sales,
    fact_soh,
    fact_sales_benchmark,
    fact_soh_benchmark,
  ]

  relationships: [
    // Dim Store -> core facts. The RLS rule enters here and can go no further:
    // one_way means RLP flows dim -> fact only, so it cannot climb back out of these facts.
    relationship(fact_sales.store_key > dim_store.store_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_soh.store_key > dim_store.store_key, true, 'one_way'),

    // Dim Region -> core facts.
    relationship(fact_sales.region_key > dim_region.region_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_soh.region_key > dim_region.region_key, true, 'one_way'),

    // Dim Date -> Fact Sales and Fact Sales (Benchmark). Neither SOH fact connects.
    // This is the edge that would bridge core -> benchmark if it were two_way. It is not.
    relationship(fact_sales.date_key > dim_date.date_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_sales_benchmark.date_key > dim_date.date_key, true, 'one_way'),

    // Dim Region (Benchmark) -> benchmark facts. Nothing here leads back to Dim Store.
    relationship(fact_sales_benchmark.region_key > dim_region_benchmark.region_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_soh_benchmark.region_key > dim_region_benchmark.region_key, true, 'one_way'),
  ]

  // vendor_id stands in for the customer's ga_sr_storeid, which does not exist in this workspace.
  permission store_reporting_proprietor_permissions {
    field: r(dim_store.store_key)
    operator: 'matches_user_attribute'
    value: 'vendor_id'
  }
}
